Project Code Quality & Standards

- Language Usage:
All user-facing text in the application must use British English spelling and conventions.

- Code Cleanliness:
Do not leave comments, console.log statements, or any debugging code in the codebase unless explicitly requested.

- State Management:
Minimise use of useState and useEffect. Prefer a data-driven architecture where possible.

- Query-Centric Approach:
Use TanStack Query cache as the single source of truth for server state. Avoid duplicating server data in local state.

- UI Reactivity:
Components should be reactive to query state. Avoid imperative state handling and manual data syncing.

- Consistency & Simplicity:
Follow consistent patterns throughout the codebase.
Prioritise clarity, predictability, and simplicity over cleverness.
Do not overcomplicate code or solutions — write simple, reliable, and maintainable code.

- TypeScript Best Practices:
Always use proper typing; avoid any type where possible. Utilize TypeScript's utility types when appropriate (Pick, Omit, etc.). Define interfaces for all component props and state.

- Code Organization:
Group related components and hooks in feature-based directories. Follow consistent import ordering (external → internal). Maintain a clear separation between UI and business logic.

- Component Structure:
Keep components small and focused. If it has more than one responsibility, split it.

- Reusability:
Abstract repeated logic into reusable hooks, utils, or shared components. Don't reinvent the wheel for each feature.

- TanStack First-Class Usage:
Use TanStack Router for routing with file-based structure. Prefer TanStack Query for all data fetching and caching. Follow best practices:
Use loader APIs to fetch data on route-level. 
Co-locate queries with the routes/components that consume them. 
Use queryClient.invalidateQueries for refresh logic instead of manual state hacks. 
Keep route modules clean by separating logic (e.g. useQuery, mutations) into dedicated hooks. 
Embrace "cache-first" patterns — if you're fetching data with useEffect, you're doing it wrong.

- Helper Abstractions:
Extract reusable logic into helper functions, custom hooks, or utility files. This includes:
Data transformations and formatting.
Shared hook logic (e.g. pagination, query options, mutation patterns).
API wrappers or service-layer logic.
Schema validations and default value factories.
Don't repeat yourself — if it feels reusable, make it modular.

- UI Components with shadcn/ui:
Use the shadcn/ui component library for all UI elements. Prefer extending or composing existing shadcn components over building from scratch.
Do not import random UI libs or roll your own buttons because you're feeling "creative".
Custom components should follow the same structure and styling as shadcn.
Consistency, accessibility, and theme adherence are non-negotiable.


